-- title:
--   Extension criterion for points along a p-isogeny with augmentation
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $K$ be commutative rings equipped with $\mathbb Z$-algebra structures, let $\iota \colon A \to K$ be a $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphism, $\varepsilon \colon K \to \mathbb Z$ a $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphism, $p$ a natural number and $a$ an element of $K$. Assume: $\iota$ is injective; $K$ has no $p$-torsion, i.e. $p k = 0$ forces $k = 0$ for $k \in K$; $a - 1$ lies in the ideal of $K$ generated by $p$; $a \cdot (k - \varepsilon(k) \cdot 1) = 0$ for every $k \in K$, where $\varepsilon(k)$ is pushed into $K$ along the structure map; and for every $k \in K$ there exist $n \in \mathbb N$ and $h \in A$ with $p^n k = \iota(h)$. Then for every commutative ring $R$ both of the following hold: first, precomposition with $\iota$ is injective on $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphisms $K \to R$, i.e. $f \circ \iota = g \circ \iota$ implies $f = g$; second, a $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphism $g \colon A \to R$ factors as $f \circ \iota$ for some $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphism $f \colon K \to R$ if and only if there is $b \in R$ with $b - 1$ in the ideal generated by $p$ and $b \cdot (g(h) - \varepsilon(\iota(h)) \cdot 1) = 0$ for all $h \in A$.
--
--   In geometric terms this says that $\operatorname{Spec} K \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ is a monomorphism whose $R$-points are exactly those $R$-points $g$ of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ that agree with the section $\varepsilon$ on a Zariski-open neighbourhood $D(b)$ of the closed subscheme $V(p) \subset \operatorname{Spec} R$, the pair $D(p), D(b)$ covering $\operatorname{Spec} R$. It is an elementary commutative-algebra input to [`HopfAlgebra.prime_and_exists_bialgHom_monoidAlgebra_of_natCard_algHom_eq_of_convPow_of_not_finite_of_ne_two`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.prime_and_exists_bialgHom_monoidAlgebra_of_natCard_algHom_eq_of_convPow_of_not_finite_of_ne_two), where points of such punctured neighbourhoods of a unit section are counted.
